export default "Cycle syncing became a mainstream wellness concept through Alisa Vitti's work, starting with the book WomanCode and later In the FLO. The core idea is that the menstrual cycle creates four distinct hormonal environments. Adjusting your activity to those environments may work better than treating every day as identical.\n\nThe hormonal effects on metabolism, recovery, and cognition are real and reasonably well documented. What is harder to establish is whether specific protocols (eat these seeds on Day 8, do HIIT on these days, reduce carbs in the luteal phase) produce meaningfully better outcomes than simply paying attention to your natural energy variation.\n\nHere is what we know, what we do not, and how to start.\n\n## The Four Phases and Their Hormonal Reality\n\n### Menstrual Phase (Days 1-5, or however long your period lasts)\n\n**Hormones:** Both estrogen and progesterone are at their lowest. Prostaglandins are elevated and drive uterine contractions.\n\n**What this means physically:** Energy is typically lower. The prostaglandin response can cause fatigue and pain. Iron levels may decline if flow is heavy.\n\n**What actually helps:** Rest is legitimate. Light movement such as walking, yoga, and stretching reduces prostaglandin-mediated cramps through endorphin release and improved blood flow. Intense exercise can increase flow in some people by raising prostaglandins. This varies individually. Iron-rich foods support replenishment if you lose significant blood.\n\n**What the cycle syncing framework recommends:** Rest, gentle yoga, introspective activities, iron-rich foods. This is reasonable, though the benefits come from listening to actual fatigue rather than from the phase label itself.\n\n### Follicular Phase (Post-period through ovulation)\n\n**Hormones:** Estrogen rises progressively. FSH peaks then falls. Testosterone rises.\n\n**What this means physically:** Energy improves as estrogen rises. Estrogen supports muscle protein synthesis, reduces post-exercise inflammatory markers, and improves neuromuscular coordination. Insulin sensitivity is higher. Cognitive function, including verbal fluency and working memory, tends to be better.\n\n**What actually helps:** This is the phase for testing personal records, trying new physical skills, and tackling demanding projects. The body genuinely supports higher-intensity output.\n\n**What the cycle syncing framework recommends:** Strength training, HIIT, complex carbohydrates, fermented foods, social activities. The exercise recommendations have physiological backing. The food prescriptions are more speculative.\n\n### Ovulatory Phase (3-5 days around ovulation)\n\n**Hormones:** Estrogen peaks. LH surges. Testosterone peaks.\n\n**What this means physically:** This is typically the highest-energy, most cognitively sharp point in the cycle. Testosterone and estrogen together support confidence and social engagement. Reaction time may be faster. Grip strength is measurably higher around ovulation in some studies.\n\n**What actually helps:** High-output activities, group exercise, high-stakes meetings, and creative work fit this window well. The energy spike is real.\n\n**What the cycle syncing framework recommends:** Cardio, collaborative work, raw vegetables, lighter foods. The high-output observation is well-supported. Specific food recommendations are mostly just sensible nutrition.\n\n### Luteal Phase (Post-ovulation through menstruation)\n\n**Hormones:** Progesterone rises and dominates. Estrogen has a secondary peak, then both decline sharply before menstruation.\n\n**What this means physically:** Body temperature rises by 0.2-0.5°C. This reduces heat tolerance and raises perceived exertion. Fat oxidation increases; the body preferentially burns fat. Muscle protein synthesis declines compared to the follicular phase. Carbohydrate cravings increase as insulin sensitivity dips slightly. Allopregnanolone from progesterone has a sedative effect.\n\n**What actually helps:** Reduced intensity in the late luteal phase is physiologically supported. Moderate steady-state work (running, cycling) tends to feel better than high-intensity intervals. Adequate carbohydrates support mood stability as serotonin signaling becomes more dependent on blood glucose. Magnesium helps with PMS and sleep.\n\n**What the cycle syncing framework recommends:** Yoga, walks, complex carbs, magnesium-rich foods, fewer commitments. The physiology supports a lower-output approach. Specific prescriptions vary by source.\n\n## What You Actually Need to Start\n\n1. **Track your cycle.** Know when your period starts and ends. Confirm ovulation with LH strips or BBT. The phases are defined by your actual hormonal events, not by calendar days.\n\n2. **Log energy and performance.** Rate your energy, mood, and workout quality by cycle day for 2-3 cycles. Look for your own patterns rather than trying to match a framework.\n\n3. **Adjust one variable at a time.** If you want to try reducing training intensity in the late luteal phase, try it for 2 cycles and assess. Do not change everything at once.\n\n4. **Treat it as a framework, not a prescription.** The most consistent finding from people who practice cycle syncing is that it improves attention to their body's actual signals. That attentiveness is the real benefit. The specific foods and protocols are secondary.\n\n## What This Means for Floriva Users\n\nFloriva's cycle phase tracking gives you the foundational data cycle syncing requires. Logging symptoms, energy, and workout notes by cycle phase across several months builds the personal pattern data that tells you - not a generic framework - how your specific body responds to your specific cycle phases.\n\nThat data is yours. It stays on your device. For health information this personal, that matters.\n## Medical Context\n\nThis page is educational and is not medical advice. Use it to prepare questions and track patterns; diagnosis, treatment decisions, supplement dosing, and medication use should be reviewed with a qualified clinician, especially if pain is severe, bleeding is heavy, cycles change suddenly, pregnancy is possible, or you have a known condition.";
